{%- comment -%}
  Accent Typography — the single owner of decorative accents applied to the
  <strong> (bold channel) and <em> (italic channel) spans Shopify emits inside
  a richtext / inline_richtext field, plus the whole-element
  [data-accent-target] channel.

  This snippet owns BOTH halves of the feature:
    1. the inherit-vs-custom resolution (theme Typography settings vs. the
       caller's own stored settings, gated by one checkbox), and
    2. the output — either the data-accent-* attributes for the scope element
       or the scoped CSS + JS loader.

  Callers never resolve settings themselves. They pass their settings object
  and render this snippet twice:

    <h2 id="heading-{{ block.id }}"
      {% render 'accent-typography', source: block.settings, mode: 'attributes' %}
    >{{ block.settings.heading_text }}</h2>

    {%- assign accent_selector = '#heading-' | append: block.id -%}
    {% render 'accent-typography', source: block.settings, selector: accent_selector %}

  ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  CONTRACT

  Required (css mode only):
  - selector:     {String}  CSS selector of the SCOPE element that directly
                            contains the spans (e.g. '#heading-<id>' or
                            '.block-<id> .rte').

  Resolution:
  - source:       {Object}  Caller settings (block.settings / section.settings).
                            When source.use_custom_accent_typography is true,
                            each accent_* key present on `source` wins over the
                            matching theme setting; otherwise the theme
                            Typography settings apply. Omit `source` to run on
                            theme settings alone.
  Output selection:
  - mode:         {String}  'css' (default) — emits the scoped {% style %} and,
                            unless assets:false, the accent JS loader.
                            'attributes' — emits the data-accent-* attribute
                            string for the scope element (nothing when every
                            channel is 'none').
  - assets:       {Boolean} css mode only. Default true. Set false when the host
                            page already loaded assets/accent-typography.js.
  - enabled:      {Boolean} Default true. Pass false to suppress all output
                            (e.g. vertical headings, which use writing-mode).

  Style enum:
    none, color, italic, highlight, highlight_marker, underline,
    underline_sketch, underline_wavy, circle, circle_sketch, oval, oval_sketch

  Setting ids read from `source` (and their theme counterparts):
    use_custom_accent_typography (gate), accent_use_font, accent_bold_style,
    accent_italic_style, accent_trigger, accent_threshold, accent_stroke_width,
    accent_duration, accent_reverse, accent_loop, accent_loop_mode.
    accent_effect is read from `source` only and supplies the target channel.

  Colors come from the active color scheme (var(--color-accent) /
  var(--color-accent-contrast)) — never a raw picker. All motion is driven by a
  single normalised custom property, --accent-progress (0 = hidden/empty,
  1 = fully drawn), so CSS accents and JS-injected SVG accents stay in lockstep
  under both the "play once in view" and "scroll scrub" triggers. When JS is
  absent, --accent-progress defaults to 1 so accents render fully (no flash of
  hidden text).

  ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  SCHEMA CONTRACT — copy-paste into the caller's {% schema %} settings array.
  Ids and visible_if conditions must stay exactly as written; this snippet reads
  them by name. Swap `block.settings` for `section.settings` in a section.

  Shape of the group: the header and the two always-on controls
  (`accent_effect`, `use_custom_accent_typography`) carry NO visible_if; every
  control below the gate carries `{{ block.settings.use_custom_accent_typography }}`,
  and `accent_threshold` / `accent_loop` / `accent_loop_mode` append their own
  term to it. A header that only labels custom controls carries the gate too, so
  the group collapses cleanly when the override is off.

  Take `accent_effect` only if the caller drives the whole-element channel (a
  Subtitle, a caption); a caller that only decorates bold/italic runs inside a
  richtext field — Heading, Rich Text — omits it and keeps
  accent_bold_style / accent_italic_style instead.

  `accent_selected` is NOT part of this contract — it belongs to
  snippets/accent-resolver.liquid — but a caller that can draw an accent effect
  must leave it UNGATED (no `color_style == 'accent'` visible_if), because the
  decoration reads --color-accent whatever colour the text itself takes.

    {
